In seismic imaging, the seismic reflectivity model is a crucial component that requires to be deconvolved with a blurring matrix, H, which is often large, underdetermined, and poorly conditioned due to its block-circant nature. Deconvolving the reflectivity model directly from the inverse of the blurring matrix is not feasible due to its ill-conditioning and the added noise from seismic data, which necessitates a sparsity-promoting deconvolution approach. However, conventional sparse methods are unsuitable for non-uniformly sparse data, which is present in seismic imaging. To address this limitation, we propose a novel algorithm, NuSPAN, which is a network-based algorithm that learns a non-uniform sparse pattern from data and uses it to deconvolve the reflectivity model through proximal optimization. The proposed algorithm is evaluated using synthetic data from the Sigsbee-2A and Balderin datasets and real data from the Atlantic Canada margin, which demonstrate its superiority over existing methods in terms of reconstruction accuracy and the ability to learn a non-uniform sparse pattern. The source code for NuSPAN is publicly available on GitHub. \url{https://github.com/chenxuanyu/NuSPAN} 
